  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is partnering with Fiji&#x2019;s Ministry of Health and Medical Services (MHMS) to improve the quality and accessibility of health services. Through the Australia-Fiji Health Program, Australia is providing technical assistance to support MHMS implement its National Disability Inclusive Health and Rehabilitation Action Plan 2023-2027 and supporting local Organisations of Persons with Disabilities to conduct disability access audits of health facilities. Partnerships with the Spinal Injury Association of Fiji and the Fiji Association of the Deaf have enhanced support services for individuals with disabilities accessing healthcare at Colonial War Memorial Hospital, Fiji&#x2019;s oldest and main referral hospital. In 2024, 10 patients accessed interpretation services and 84 wheelchairs were repaired.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is supporting Fiji to respond to the threat of HIV.  In 2024, Australia provided 5,560 point-of-care HIV test kits to address critical shortages in Fiji to bolster screening and early detection. Australia also engaged specialist technical advisors to assist the Government of Fiji with more accurate estimates of the number of HIV cases in Fiji and in developing its plan to respond. These efforts helped inform the Government of Fiji&#x2019;s declaration of an HIV outbreak and supported the release of its national HIV response plan.
&#x200d;
Since the declaration of the outbreak, Australia has provided an additional AUD3.9 million to support Fiji to implement its HIV response. This funding is providing Fiji with urgent technical assistance in the areas of prevention; treatment and care; diagnostics and data.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through the Fiji Health Program, partners with the Ministry of Health and Medical Services to support critical supply chain, digital health, and patient safety and quality care reforms. This included support to the national roll-out of mSupply, a logistics information system, to all health facilities in Fiji. The use of mSupply has significantly reduced order processing times and increased the availability of essential medicines. In 2023, Australia also supported the development of Fiji&#x2019;s five-year Digital Health Strategy, which aims to improve healthcare quality by strengthening Fiji&#x2019;s health information systems.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through the Fiji Health Program, supported the Ministry of Health and Medical Services (MHMS) respond to the carbapenem-resistant organism &apos;super-bug&apos; outbreak at CWM Hospital, beginning in January 2023. Australia funded a &#x2018;deep clean&#x2019; of operating theatres, the emergency procurement of critical infection prevention and control (IPC) consumables (including alcohol hand rub and anti-bacterial wipes) and IPC training for ward assistants and cleaners. In 2023, Australia also procured 20,000 HIV and Syphilis Point of Care Duo Testing Kits to aid MHMS in the rapid detection and early treatment of sexually transmitted infections such as HIV and syphilis.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
